1
00:00:00,096 --> 00:00:01,668
You wanted to be a poet.

2
00:00:01,668 --> 00:00:04,447
A seat near the jarl's high table,

3
00:00:04,447 --> 00:00:07,002
a gold ring pulled from his own arm,

4
00:00:07,002 --> 00:00:08,938
and your verses repeated

5
00:00:08,938 --> 00:00:11,305
in every hall for a hundred years.

6
00:00:11,305 --> 00:00:14,513
Instead, it is the middle of the night,

7
00:00:14,513 --> 00:00:16,845
the lamp is smoking, your head hurts,

8
00:00:16,845 --> 00:00:19,806
and you need eight perfect lines by morning.

9
00:00:20,116 --> 00:00:22,438
Somebody has to find the next word.

10
00:00:22,438 --> 00:00:24,572
Tonight, that somebody is you.

11
00:00:24,572 --> 00:00:26,167
Imagine you are Brand,

12
00:00:26,167 --> 00:00:30,253
Bjorn's nephew, from the valley of our earlier chapters.

13
00:00:30,253 --> 00:00:32,125
Like Eirik and Aslaug,

14
00:00:32,125 --> 00:00:33,659
you are an imagined person,

15
00:00:33,659 --> 00:00:35,715
built from what poems, sagas,

16
00:00:35,715 --> 00:00:39,892
laws and scholars tell us, not a real man from history.

17
00:00:39,892 --> 00:00:42,046
You are a slim young man with long

18
00:00:42,046 --> 00:00:44,810
light-brown hair tied at the neck,

19
00:00:44,810 --> 00:00:47,899
no beard yet, a pale blue tunic,

20
00:00:47,899 --> 00:00:51,987
a grey-green cloak, and one thin silver ring on your arm.

21
00:00:51,987 --> 00:00:53,295
It is the first thing

22
00:00:53,295 --> 00:00:54,902
poetry ever paid you.

23
00:00:55,340 --> 00:00:57,433
You polish it more than your shoes.

24
00:00:57,433 --> 00:00:58,950
Popular culture imagines

25
00:00:58,950 --> 00:01:01,140
Viking poets as cheerful bards,

26
00:01:01,140 --> 00:01:03,128
strumming a harp at a feast.

27
00:01:03,128 --> 00:01:05,488
The real court poets of the north,

28
00:01:05,488 --> 00:01:08,372
the skalds, worked in one of the most

29
00:01:08,372 --> 00:01:11,089
demanding styles of poetry ever invented.

30
00:01:11,639 --> 00:01:13,786
It was a job with real money,

31
00:01:13,786 --> 00:01:16,401
real danger and very real enemies.

32
00:01:16,401 --> 00:01:18,549
And at the end of this story,

33
00:01:18,922 --> 00:01:21,886
we will read a stone on the Swedish island of

34
00:01:21,886 --> 00:01:24,529
Öland that carries the only complete

35
00:01:24,529 --> 00:01:27,145
skaldic stanza ever found on a runestone.

36
00:01:27,727 --> 00:01:29,917
It starts with Bjorn's voyages.

37
00:01:29,917 --> 00:01:32,137
All your life, you have heard him

38
00:01:32,137 --> 00:01:34,214
tell the same stories at the hearth:

39
00:01:34,214 --> 00:01:37,087
the storm, the raid, the silver,

40
00:01:37,087 --> 00:01:41,117
the cut on Eirik's arm that he swears was heroic.

41
00:01:41,117 --> 00:01:42,561
One winter,

42
00:01:42,561 --> 00:01:44,457
you put the stories into verse.

43
00:01:44,457 --> 00:01:46,534
People stop eating to listen.

44
00:01:47,212 --> 00:01:49,197
Bjorn pretends not to be moved,

45
00:01:49,197 --> 00:01:51,702
then asks you to say it again,

46
00:01:51,702 --> 00:01:53,739
louder, with more about him.

47
00:01:53,739 --> 00:01:55,230
That spring,

48
00:01:55,230 --> 00:01:58,293
a jarl's man passing through the valley hears you.

49
00:01:58,293 --> 00:02:00,883
The jarl in the north likes poets,

50
00:02:00,883 --> 00:02:01,419
he says.

51
00:02:01,419 --> 00:02:03,344
Come and try your luck.

52
00:02:03,344 --> 00:02:04,526
Your mother cries.

53
00:02:04,526 --> 00:02:07,252
Bjorn gives you his second-best cloak.

54
00:02:07,818 --> 00:02:10,129
Aslaug gives you bread for the road and a

55
00:02:10,129 --> 00:02:13,171
look that says: come back with your head.

56
00:02:13,171 --> 00:02:14,868
The form you will be judged on

57
00:02:14,868 --> 00:02:17,286
is called dróttkvætt, court metre.

58
00:02:17,286 --> 00:02:19,552
The scholar Margaret Clunies Ross

59
00:02:19,552 --> 00:02:22,622
calls it the commonest metre of skaldic verse,

60
00:02:22,622 --> 00:02:23,786
and explains that its

61
00:02:23,786 --> 00:02:25,594
name means something like:

62
00:02:25,594 --> 00:02:28,221
that which is recited before the retinue.

63
00:02:28,221 --> 00:02:29,774
The rules are brutal.

64
00:02:29,774 --> 00:02:32,011
Hurstwic, a history site that

65
00:02:32,011 --> 00:02:34,669
studies the sagas, sets them out.

66
00:02:34,669 --> 00:02:36,966
Each stanza has eight lines.

67
00:02:36,966 --> 00:02:38,750
Each line has six syllables,

68
00:02:38,750 --> 00:02:40,371
three of them stressed,

69
00:02:40,745 --> 00:02:43,022
and it must end on an unstressed one.

70
00:02:43,022 --> 00:02:44,978
The lines come in pairs bound

71
00:02:44,978 --> 00:02:46,669
together by alliteration,

72
00:02:46,669 --> 00:02:49,737
and every single line needs an internal rhyme.

73
00:02:49,737 --> 00:02:51,889
The rules were so tight that,

74
00:02:51,889 --> 00:02:54,556
as Hurstwic notes, poets often shuffled the

75
00:02:54,556 --> 00:02:57,551
normal word order just to make the metre work.

76
00:02:57,551 --> 00:02:59,391
Listeners had to untangle the sentence

77
00:02:59,391 --> 00:03:02,663
in their heads, at speed, while drinking.

78
00:03:02,663 --> 00:03:04,587
So imagine a crossword,

79
00:03:04,587 --> 00:03:06,483
a riddle and a rap battle,

80
00:03:06,483 --> 00:03:09,223
all at once, performed in front of armed men

81
00:03:09,223 --> 00:03:11,269
who have been drinking since noon.

82
00:03:11,723 --> 00:03:13,115
That is the job.

83
00:03:13,115 --> 00:03:14,507
The art was old.

84
00:03:14,507 --> 00:03:17,168
The earliest skald whose verses

85
00:03:17,168 --> 00:03:18,668
survive is Bragi the Old,

86
00:03:18,668 --> 00:03:20,640
a Norwegian of the early eight hundreds.

87
00:03:20,640 --> 00:03:23,173
But after Iceland was settled,

88
00:03:23,173 --> 00:03:24,547
Clunies Ross notes,

89
00:03:24,547 --> 00:03:27,905
Icelanders increasingly took over the craft,

90
00:03:27,905 --> 00:03:30,039
and made themselves its specialists.

91
00:03:30,039 --> 00:03:31,693
Somewhere in Iceland,

92
00:03:31,693 --> 00:03:34,970
Grim's grandchildren may yet put you out of work.

93
00:03:34,970 --> 00:03:36,944
Then there are the kennings.

94
00:03:36,944 --> 00:03:39,977
A kenning is a poetic name for something.

95
00:03:40,368 --> 00:03:42,050
You never say the plain word if

96
00:03:42,050 --> 00:03:43,732
you can say something stranger.

97
00:03:43,732 --> 00:03:45,548
A ship becomes a wave's horse.

98
00:03:45,548 --> 00:03:48,159
A warrior becomes a feeder of ravens,

99
00:03:48,159 --> 00:03:50,800
because of what he leaves on the battlefield.

100
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:53,444
One poem calls blood the sea of wounds.

101
00:03:53,444 --> 00:03:55,307
Every skaldic battle is,

102
00:03:55,307 --> 00:03:58,544
by definition, a very good day for the ravens.

103
00:03:58,544 --> 00:04:00,808
In one poem quoted in his saga,

104
00:04:00,808 --> 00:04:03,818
Egil, a poet we will meet shortly,

105
00:04:03,818 --> 00:04:05,030
calls the English king

106
00:04:05,030 --> 00:04:07,490
Athelstan the feeder of the battle-hawk.

107
00:04:07,832 --> 00:04:09,596
Gold has dozens of names.

108
00:04:09,596 --> 00:04:11,830
In his handbook for poets,

109
00:04:11,830 --> 00:04:13,393
written in the twelve hundreds,

110
00:04:13,393 --> 00:04:17,463
the Icelander Snorri Sturluson lists some of them:

111
00:04:17,463 --> 00:04:21,816
Sif's hair, Freyja's tears, the seed of the Fyris plains.

112
00:04:21,816 --> 00:04:23,818
To understand that last one,

113
00:04:24,191 --> 00:04:26,129
you need to know a whole legend.

114
00:04:26,129 --> 00:04:27,603
That was the point.

115
00:04:28,218 --> 00:04:29,552
Kennings demanded a deep

116
00:04:29,552 --> 00:04:31,774
knowledge of Norse myth and legend.

117
00:04:32,308 --> 00:04:34,118
A good poem flattered the patron

118
00:04:34,118 --> 00:04:36,224
and the audience at the same time:

119
00:04:36,224 --> 00:04:38,848
only clever people could follow it.

120
00:04:39,302 --> 00:04:41,925
Snorri, a Christian writing more than

121
00:04:41,925 --> 00:04:44,525
two centuries after the conversion,

122
00:04:44,525 --> 00:04:46,917
addressed his handbook to young skalds who

123
00:04:46,917 --> 00:04:49,595
wanted to learn the old language of poetry,

124
00:04:49,595 --> 00:04:51,538
and told them to master his book.

125
00:04:52,042 --> 00:04:53,110
In the same breath,

126
00:04:53,110 --> 00:04:56,132
he warned them not to believe in the heathen gods.

127
00:04:56,539 --> 00:04:57,235
By his time,

128
00:04:57,235 --> 00:04:59,891
the old myths had become a kind of grammar.

129
00:05:00,217 --> 00:05:02,540
Poetry itself had kennings too:

130
00:05:02,540 --> 00:05:06,765
Odin's drink, Odin's gift, Kvasir's blood.

131
00:05:07,301 --> 00:05:09,406
Snorri tells the myth behind them.

132
00:05:09,406 --> 00:05:12,422
Odin stole the mead of poetry from a giant,

133
00:05:12,422 --> 00:05:14,363
turned himself into an eagle,

134
00:05:14,689 --> 00:05:17,190
flew home, and spat the mead into jars

135
00:05:17,190 --> 00:05:19,742
that the gods had set out in their yard.

136
00:05:19,742 --> 00:05:21,964
But the giant was close behind.

137
00:05:22,401 --> 00:05:24,800
So, in his hurry, Odin let some of

138
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:27,550
the mead escape out of his back end.

139
00:05:27,550 --> 00:05:29,254
Nobody bothered to collect it,

140
00:05:29,254 --> 00:05:30,268
Snorri says.

141
00:05:30,722 --> 00:05:32,853
Anyone who wanted it could have it.

142
00:05:32,853 --> 00:05:33,977
And that, he says,

143
00:05:33,977 --> 00:05:37,995
is called the share of the poetasters: the bad poets.

144
00:05:38,561 --> 00:05:39,786
Every skald believes

145
00:05:39,786 --> 00:05:41,185
he drank from the jars.

146
00:05:41,185 --> 00:05:43,114
Every skald suspects his

147
00:05:43,114 --> 00:05:44,844
rivals drank from the other part.

148
00:05:44,844 --> 00:05:46,014
The jarl's hall is the

149
00:05:46,014 --> 00:05:48,434
biggest building you have ever seen.

150
00:05:48,434 --> 00:05:50,914
Long benches, a fire down the middle,

151
00:05:50,914 --> 00:05:53,445
shields on the walls, and a hundred men who

152
00:05:53,445 --> 00:05:56,653
all seem to be related to someone important.

153
00:05:56,653 --> 00:05:58,914
Skalds worked mainly at the courts of

154
00:05:58,914 --> 00:06:01,612
Norwegian kings and sometimes of jarls.

155
00:06:01,612 --> 00:06:03,811
Their main business was praise.

156
00:06:03,811 --> 00:06:05,788
The National Museum of Denmark

157
00:06:05,788 --> 00:06:07,881
notes that skaldic poems praise

158
00:06:07,881 --> 00:06:10,190
kings and magnates who gave gold

159
00:06:10,190 --> 00:06:12,198
rings to those who fought for them.

160
00:06:12,747 --> 00:06:15,386
Clunies Ross puts it simply:

161
00:06:15,386 --> 00:06:19,038
patrons rewarded their poets for effective praise.

162
00:06:19,038 --> 00:06:20,823
Your first poem is about a battle

163
00:06:20,823 --> 00:06:22,536
the jarl fought the summer before.

164
00:06:22,536 --> 00:06:23,815
You were not there.

165
00:06:24,284 --> 00:06:24,764
It was,

166
00:06:24,764 --> 00:06:27,581
by most accounts, a short, muddy skirmish.

167
00:06:27,890 --> 00:06:28,747
In your poem,

168
00:06:28,747 --> 00:06:30,583
the ravens are very well fed.

169
00:06:30,583 --> 00:06:32,548
The jarl listens without moving.

170
00:06:32,548 --> 00:06:33,893
Then he pulls a ring from

171
00:06:33,893 --> 00:06:36,003
his arm and tosses it to you.

172
00:06:36,003 --> 00:06:38,113
It is heavier than your whole cloak.

173
00:06:38,113 --> 00:06:39,580
The hall cheers.

174
00:06:39,580 --> 00:06:40,748
A rival poet stops

175
00:06:40,748 --> 00:06:42,285
cheering rather quickly.

176
00:06:42,285 --> 00:06:43,531
He is an Icelander,

177
00:06:43,531 --> 00:06:46,209
of course: a sharp-nosed man in a black cloak

178
00:06:46,209 --> 00:06:48,075
who knows a hundred names for gold

179
00:06:48,075 --> 00:06:50,656
and uses all of them in every poem.

180
00:06:51,062 --> 00:06:53,282
He nods at your ring and says nothing.

181
00:06:53,282 --> 00:06:54,992
In a hall full of warriors,

182
00:06:54,992 --> 00:06:57,657
he is the one you are most afraid of.

183
00:06:58,209 --> 00:06:59,407
Life at court is less

184
00:06:59,407 --> 00:07:00,823
glamorous than the poems.

185
00:07:00,823 --> 00:07:02,865
You sleep on a bench near the door,

186
00:07:02,865 --> 00:07:04,736
eat at the far end of the table,

187
00:07:04,736 --> 00:07:08,538
and spend a great deal of time watching the jarl's face.

188
00:07:08,538 --> 00:07:10,298
A good mood means a ring.

189
00:07:10,298 --> 00:07:12,428
A bad mood means you are

190
00:07:12,428 --> 00:07:15,169
suddenly very interested in the fire.

191
00:07:15,169 --> 00:07:17,750
But there was a limit to flattery.

192
00:07:17,750 --> 00:07:19,313
In the prologue to his history

193
00:07:19,313 --> 00:07:21,020
of the kings of Norway,

194
00:07:21,020 --> 00:07:23,918
Snorri explains why he trusted these poems.

195
00:07:24,291 --> 00:07:25,289
Poets, he says,

196
00:07:25,289 --> 00:07:28,992
praise most the man they are standing in front of.

197
00:07:28,992 --> 00:07:32,148
But no one would dare to tell a chief,

198
00:07:32,148 --> 00:07:33,075
to his face,

199
00:07:33,075 --> 00:07:37,124
about deeds that everyone listening knew were false.

200
00:07:37,124 --> 00:07:40,224
That would be mockery, not praise.

201
00:07:40,224 --> 00:07:43,289
Modern scholars are less trusting,

202
00:07:43,289 --> 00:07:44,509
and argue about how

203
00:07:44,509 --> 00:07:46,762
far the poems can be relied on.

204
00:07:47,360 --> 00:07:49,855
But you learn Snorri's rule fast:

205
00:07:49,855 --> 00:07:52,927
exaggerate if you must, but never invent.

206
00:07:52,927 --> 00:07:54,179
Everybody in the hall

207
00:07:54,179 --> 00:07:55,704
was at that skirmish.

208
00:07:55,704 --> 00:07:58,052
Everybody remembers who ran.

209
00:07:58,052 --> 00:07:59,884
The old men at court tell

210
00:07:59,884 --> 00:08:01,840
you a story to make the point.

211
00:08:01,840 --> 00:08:04,602
Only a few years ago, the saga says,

212
00:08:04,602 --> 00:08:06,699
a veteran poet named Eyvind,

213
00:08:06,699 --> 00:08:08,801
who had served the generous King Hákon,

214
00:08:08,801 --> 00:08:12,191
made a verse about the new kings, the sons of Gunnhild.

215
00:08:12,191 --> 00:08:14,368
He mocked them for burying

216
00:08:14,368 --> 00:08:16,042
their gold in the ground instead

217
00:08:16,042 --> 00:08:18,067
of giving it away, as Hákon had.

218
00:08:18,442 --> 00:08:20,634
King Harald, one of those sons,

219
00:08:20,634 --> 00:08:23,627
summoned him and accused him of disloyalty.

220
00:08:23,627 --> 00:08:26,550
Eyvind had to hand over his great gold ring,

221
00:08:26,550 --> 00:08:28,490
called Molde, as a fine.

222
00:08:29,041 --> 00:08:31,200
He went home, and the saga says nothing

223
00:08:31,200 --> 00:08:33,703
of him ever coming near that king again.

224
00:08:33,703 --> 00:08:35,214
The lesson was clear:

225
00:08:35,214 --> 00:08:37,895
the king's gold was for praising the king.

226
00:08:37,895 --> 00:08:39,664
Not every poet got a ring.

227
00:08:40,119 --> 00:08:41,711
Some got a death sentence.

228
00:08:41,711 --> 00:08:43,792
The most famous escape belongs to

229
00:08:43,792 --> 00:08:47,834
the Icelandic poet and Viking Egil Skallagrímsson,

230
00:08:48,242 --> 00:08:50,210
not long before your time.

231
00:08:50,210 --> 00:08:52,993
His saga, written down in the twelve hundreds,

232
00:08:52,993 --> 00:08:55,465
says that Egil was shipwrecked

233
00:08:55,465 --> 00:08:58,540
in Northumbria and fell into the hands of his enemy,

234
00:08:58,540 --> 00:09:00,625
King Eirik Bloodaxe, at York.

235
00:09:00,625 --> 00:09:03,628
A friend advised him to stay up all

236
00:09:03,628 --> 00:09:05,600
night and compose a great praise poem

237
00:09:05,600 --> 00:09:07,932
for the very king who had condemned him.

238
00:09:07,932 --> 00:09:10,534
In the morning, Egil recited it.

239
00:09:10,534 --> 00:09:12,085
According to the saga,

240
00:09:12,085 --> 00:09:15,113
the king was so impressed that he let Egil live.

241
00:09:15,113 --> 00:09:17,585
The poem is called Höfuðlausn:

242
00:09:17,585 --> 00:09:18,963
the Head-Ransom.

243
00:09:18,963 --> 00:09:20,985
It was not a one-off.

244
00:09:20,985 --> 00:09:23,088
Clunies Ross explains that patrons

245
00:09:23,088 --> 00:09:25,965
rewarded poets for praise, but a poet who

246
00:09:25,965 --> 00:09:28,861
overstepped the limits of what they would

247
00:09:28,861 --> 00:09:31,266
tolerate might find himself begging for his life.

248
00:09:31,721 --> 00:09:34,352
The Head-Ransom became a whole kind of poem,

249
00:09:34,352 --> 00:09:36,439
written to calm a furious patron.

250
00:09:36,439 --> 00:09:38,372
The same saga claims that Egil

251
00:09:38,372 --> 00:09:40,903
composed his first poem at the age of three.

252
00:09:40,903 --> 00:09:42,494
Your mother says that you

253
00:09:42,494 --> 00:09:45,119
composed your first poem at three as well.

254
00:09:45,119 --> 00:09:46,274
It was about a sheep.

255
00:09:46,274 --> 00:09:48,133
It did not save anyone's head.

256
00:09:48,133 --> 00:09:50,737
Which brings us back to tonight.

257
00:09:50,737 --> 00:09:52,254
At supper, you made a joke

258
00:09:52,254 --> 00:09:53,828
about the jarl's new horse.

259
00:09:53,828 --> 00:09:55,164
It was a good joke.

260
00:09:55,164 --> 00:09:56,817
The jarl did not laugh.

261
00:09:56,817 --> 00:09:58,602
Now he wants a poem in its

262
00:09:58,602 --> 00:10:00,363
honour by morning, and you have a

263
00:10:00,363 --> 00:10:02,940
strong feeling that this is a test.

264
00:10:02,940 --> 00:10:05,085
You work until the lamp goes out.

265
00:10:05,085 --> 00:10:07,247
By dawn, the horse is the swiftest

266
00:10:07,247 --> 00:10:10,015
steed of the north, the terror of the wave's horses,

267
00:10:10,015 --> 00:10:11,750
and a credit to its owner.

268
00:10:12,253 --> 00:10:13,323
The jarl listens,

269
00:10:13,323 --> 00:10:15,346
nods once, and says nothing.

270
00:10:15,865 --> 00:10:18,078
You will take nothing as a yes.

271
00:10:18,078 --> 00:10:19,553
Praise was safe.

272
00:10:19,959 --> 00:10:21,774
Mockery was dangerous.

273
00:10:21,774 --> 00:10:24,539
Clunies Ross points out that a

274
00:10:24,539 --> 00:10:26,393
skald's role could also stretch to

275
00:10:26,393 --> 00:10:28,657
social and political criticism,

276
00:10:28,657 --> 00:10:31,616
and some scholars even think the word skald

277
00:10:31,616 --> 00:10:34,421
is related to the English word scold.

278
00:10:34,908 --> 00:10:35,716
Icelandic law,

279
00:10:35,716 --> 00:10:39,100
written down later in the law collection called Grágás,

280
00:10:39,100 --> 00:10:41,357
took words very seriously.

281
00:10:41,795 --> 00:10:43,591
Clunies Ross shows that it was

282
00:10:43,591 --> 00:10:46,664
both explicit and severe about verbal offences,

283
00:10:46,664 --> 00:10:48,885
especially in poetic form.

284
00:10:48,885 --> 00:10:51,978
A lampoon, a mocking poem about someone,

285
00:10:51,978 --> 00:10:54,077
could be punished with outlawry.

286
00:10:54,494 --> 00:10:56,559
In the same section of the law,

287
00:10:56,559 --> 00:10:59,887
a love poem about a woman carried the same punishment,

288
00:10:59,887 --> 00:11:00,782
because of what

289
00:11:00,782 --> 00:11:03,391
it implied about her and her family.

290
00:11:03,391 --> 00:11:05,001
A single clever verse could

291
00:11:05,001 --> 00:11:07,132
cost a man everything he owned.

292
00:11:07,132 --> 00:11:07,883
And yet,

293
00:11:07,883 --> 00:11:11,996
poets used insults as weapons, even against kings.

294
00:11:13,222 --> 00:11:14,838
A saga written in the twelve

295
00:11:14,838 --> 00:11:17,244
hundreds says that when an Icelandic ship

296
00:11:17,244 --> 00:11:19,500
was stranded on the coast of Denmark,

297
00:11:19,500 --> 00:11:22,144
the Danes seized its cargo as wreck.

298
00:11:22,144 --> 00:11:24,658
The Icelanders responded by

299
00:11:24,658 --> 00:11:26,367
passing a law that lampoons should

300
00:11:26,367 --> 00:11:28,367
be composed about the Danish king,

301
00:11:28,367 --> 00:11:30,957
another Harald, and his steward.

302
00:11:30,957 --> 00:11:32,371
How many lampoons?

303
00:11:32,371 --> 00:11:34,512
It depends on the translation.

304
00:11:34,512 --> 00:11:36,983
One English version says one lampoon

305
00:11:36,983 --> 00:11:39,816
for every headland in the king's country.

306
00:11:40,269 --> 00:11:41,468
Another says one for

307
00:11:41,468 --> 00:11:43,196
every person in Iceland.

308
00:11:43,714 --> 00:11:44,407
Either way,

309
00:11:44,407 --> 00:11:46,786
it was a great deal of rude poetry.

310
00:11:47,319 --> 00:11:48,619
The saga adds that Harald

311
00:11:48,619 --> 00:11:50,070
was so angry that he planned

312
00:11:50,070 --> 00:11:52,207
to sail to Iceland in revenge.

313
00:11:52,207 --> 00:11:54,613
First, it says, he sent a wizard there in

314
00:11:54,613 --> 00:11:57,292
the shape of a whale, to scout the coast.

315
00:11:57,292 --> 00:11:59,968
But the land was guarded by spirits:

316
00:11:59,968 --> 00:12:00,894
a dragon,

317
00:12:00,894 --> 00:12:04,547
a great bird, a bull and a rock giant.

318
00:12:04,547 --> 00:12:06,455
The wizard reported back,

319
00:12:06,455 --> 00:12:08,053
and the king turned his fleet

320
00:12:08,053 --> 00:12:10,218
around and sailed home to Denmark.

321
00:12:10,543 --> 00:12:11,666
Those four guardians,

322
00:12:11,666 --> 00:12:14,102
as Heimskringla describes them,

323
00:12:14,102 --> 00:12:16,742
still stand on Iceland's coat of arms today.

324
00:12:17,356 --> 00:12:19,731
A row about a shipwreck and some very

325
00:12:19,731 --> 00:12:22,603
rude poems ended up on a national symbol.

326
00:12:22,603 --> 00:12:23,758
You make a note:

327
00:12:23,758 --> 00:12:26,316
never mock the man who feeds you.

328
00:12:26,316 --> 00:12:27,913
Then you make another note:

329
00:12:27,913 --> 00:12:29,357
never mock his horse.

330
00:12:29,357 --> 00:12:30,518
The Icelander,

331
00:12:30,518 --> 00:12:33,974
you notice, never makes jokes at supper.

332
00:12:33,974 --> 00:12:35,420
He saves them for verses,

333
00:12:35,420 --> 00:12:37,513
which he recites very quietly,

334
00:12:37,870 --> 00:12:40,430
to other Icelanders, far from the high table.

335
00:12:40,771 --> 00:12:42,825
Being a court poet also meant travelling,

336
00:12:42,825 --> 00:12:43,827
and travelling meant

337
00:12:43,827 --> 00:12:46,620
depending on other people's doors.

338
00:12:46,620 --> 00:12:47,902
In ten nineteen,

339
00:12:47,902 --> 00:12:51,062
the poet Sigvat Thordarson set out from Norway to

340
00:12:51,062 --> 00:12:53,441
Sweden on a mission for his king.

341
00:12:53,441 --> 00:12:56,852
The saga preserves his verses about the journey,

342
00:12:56,852 --> 00:12:58,231
and they are not happy.

343
00:12:58,231 --> 00:13:00,019
A hundred miles through the forest,

344
00:13:00,019 --> 00:13:03,015
he complains, and not a single alehouse.

345
00:13:03,533 --> 00:13:04,970
Every man in his party had

346
00:13:04,970 --> 00:13:06,866
a bleeding sore on each foot.

347
00:13:07,240 --> 00:13:09,912
At a farm called Hof, the door was bolted.

348
00:13:09,912 --> 00:13:12,656
The people inside said it was a holy time,

349
00:13:12,656 --> 00:13:14,265
and would not let him in.

350
00:13:14,622 --> 00:13:16,573
At the next farm, a woman stood in the

351
00:13:16,573 --> 00:13:18,974
doorway and told him not to come in:

352
00:13:18,974 --> 00:13:20,423
they were heathens there,

353
00:13:20,423 --> 00:13:24,185
afraid of Odin's wrath, and busy with a sacrifice to the elves.

354
00:13:24,185 --> 00:13:25,704
According to his verses,

355
00:13:25,704 --> 00:13:27,859
more farms turned him away after that,

356
00:13:27,859 --> 00:13:30,592
and the man said to be the most hospitable

357
00:13:30,592 --> 00:13:32,453
in the district only scowled at him.

358
00:13:32,453 --> 00:13:34,425
Sigvat put all of it into poetry.

359
00:13:34,425 --> 00:13:35,982
When you are a skald,

360
00:13:35,982 --> 00:13:38,782
even your bad days end up in verse.

361
00:13:38,782 --> 00:13:40,643
Your own travels are less dramatic.

362
00:13:40,643 --> 00:13:42,408
On your way home that autumn,

363
00:13:42,408 --> 00:13:44,256
you stop at Eirik's farm.

364
00:13:44,256 --> 00:13:44,736
Thora,

365
00:13:44,736 --> 00:13:48,102
who is nearly grown now, asks you to recite.

366
00:13:48,102 --> 00:13:49,713
Eirik asks you,

367
00:13:49,713 --> 00:13:51,441
politely, to leave him out of it.

368
00:13:51,441 --> 00:13:52,647
You do not.

369
00:13:52,647 --> 00:13:54,679
Skalds were also witnesses.

370
00:13:54,679 --> 00:13:57,023
The saga of King Olaf says that

371
00:13:57,023 --> 00:13:59,208
before the battle of Stiklestad,

372
00:13:59,208 --> 00:14:01,283
in ten thirty, the king asked his

373
00:14:01,283 --> 00:14:03,853
skalds to stand inside the shield wall.

374
00:14:03,853 --> 00:14:05,141
That way, he told them,

375
00:14:05,141 --> 00:14:07,855
they would see with their own eyes what happened,

376
00:14:07,855 --> 00:14:09,782
and would not have to follow other

377
00:14:09,782 --> 00:14:12,587
men's reports in what they later sang.

378
00:14:12,587 --> 00:14:13,940
One of those skalds,

379
00:14:13,940 --> 00:14:17,222
Thormod, fell at Stiklestad with his king.

380
00:14:17,222 --> 00:14:19,040
A poet was not a spectator.

381
00:14:19,040 --> 00:14:20,794
He could be the last witness,

382
00:14:20,794 --> 00:14:21,968
or one of the dead.

383
00:14:21,968 --> 00:14:24,921
That was the strange power of the job.

384
00:14:24,921 --> 00:14:25,702
Kings died.

385
00:14:25,702 --> 00:14:27,205
Halls burned.

386
00:14:27,205 --> 00:14:30,573
But a well-made stanza could survive for centuries,

387
00:14:30,573 --> 00:14:32,005
memorised and repeated,

388
00:14:32,005 --> 00:14:34,903
until someone finally wrote it down.

389
00:14:34,903 --> 00:14:37,385
Snorri says that the poems of the

390
00:14:37,385 --> 00:14:40,449
skalds at King Harald Fairhair's court,

391
00:14:40,449 --> 00:14:42,537
more than three centuries before him,

392
00:14:42,537 --> 00:14:45,339
were still known by heart in his day.

393
00:14:45,339 --> 00:14:47,711
Most surviving skaldic verse is known

394
00:14:47,711 --> 00:14:50,051
to us because later writers quoted it

395
00:14:50,051 --> 00:14:53,062
in the sagas, and in Snorri's handbook.

396
00:14:53,062 --> 00:14:55,922
A few poets did something more permanent.

397
00:14:55,922 --> 00:14:58,072
Which brings us to Öland.

398
00:14:58,672 --> 00:14:59,646
Near Karlevi,

399
00:14:59,646 --> 00:15:03,271
on that Swedish island, stands a runestone from

400
00:15:03,271 --> 00:15:04,350
the late nine hundreds,

401
00:15:04,350 --> 00:15:07,746
raised in memory of a chieftain named Sibbi.

402
00:15:07,746 --> 00:15:11,849
Part of its inscription is a full stanza in dróttkvætt,

403
00:15:11,849 --> 00:15:12,589
court metre,

404
00:15:12,589 --> 00:15:16,557
with kennings for a chieftain built on the names of gods.

405
00:15:16,557 --> 00:15:19,161
It is the only complete skaldic

406
00:15:19,161 --> 00:15:21,470
stanza ever found on a runestone.

407
00:15:21,470 --> 00:15:24,019
Someone paid to have a court poem

408
00:15:24,019 --> 00:15:25,740
carved in stone, so that no one

409
00:15:25,740 --> 00:15:28,417
would ever have to remember it again.

410
00:15:28,417 --> 00:15:30,232
So why was being a Viking

411
00:15:30,232 --> 00:15:31,777
skald worse than you think?

412
00:15:31,777 --> 00:15:33,823
Because the rules were brutal,

413
00:15:33,823 --> 00:15:35,109
the audience was armed,

414
00:15:35,109 --> 00:15:37,200
and your patron's mood decided whether

415
00:15:37,200 --> 00:15:39,784
you got a ring or a death sentence.

416
00:15:39,784 --> 00:15:40,894
You could not lie,

417
00:15:40,894 --> 00:15:43,948
you could not mock, and you could not write a

418
00:15:43,948 --> 00:15:46,082
love poem without risking outlawry.

419
00:15:46,488 --> 00:15:46,984
And yet,

420
00:15:46,984 --> 00:15:49,817
your words could outlast the men you praised.

421
00:15:50,319 --> 00:15:51,208
Kings are dust.

422
00:15:51,208 --> 00:15:53,670
Some of the poems are still being read,

423
00:15:53,670 --> 00:15:55,097
a thousand years later.

424
00:15:55,097 --> 00:15:57,861
That winter, at Bjorn's hearth,

425
00:15:57,861 --> 00:16:00,631
you recite your new poem about Bjorn's voyages.

426
00:16:00,631 --> 00:16:01,932
The storm is bigger,

427
00:16:01,932 --> 00:16:04,718
the silver is brighter, and Eirik's arm is

428
00:16:04,718 --> 00:16:07,256
wounded in the most heroic way possible.

429
00:16:07,256 --> 00:16:09,366
Everybody knows the truth.

430
00:16:09,366 --> 00:16:10,161
Nobody minds.

431
00:16:10,161 --> 00:16:11,468
Thora helps you with a

432
00:16:11,468 --> 00:16:13,768
difficult line about the storm.

433
00:16:13,768 --> 00:16:14,890
Her kenning for the

434
00:16:14,890 --> 00:16:16,349
sea is better than yours.

435
00:16:16,349 --> 00:16:17,640
You tell her so,

436
00:16:17,640 --> 00:16:20,336
which no court poet has ever said to a rival,

437
00:16:20,336 --> 00:16:22,502
and she never lets you forget it.

438
00:16:22,502 --> 00:16:23,313
Next spring,

439
00:16:23,313 --> 00:16:25,993
Bjorn will talk about another voyage.

440
00:16:25,993 --> 00:16:26,548
This time,

441
00:16:26,548 --> 00:16:28,711
he will ask you to come along and watch.

442
00:16:28,711 --> 00:16:30,545
And somewhere behind the house,

443
00:16:30,545 --> 00:16:32,640
the sheep has escaped again,

444
00:16:32,640 --> 00:16:36,550
which is, you decide, the start of a very promising poem.

445
00:16:36,550 --> 00:16:38,471
This is Viking Chapters,

446
00:16:38,471 --> 00:16:40,811
the lives behind the legends.

447
00:16:40,811 --> 00:16:42,166
If you would like to see another

448
00:16:42,166 --> 00:16:43,642
life behind the legends,

449
00:16:43,642 --> 00:16:46,496
subscribe, and we will meet in the next chapter.
